            <h3>Trend 3: Private Credit and SPVs Fueling the AI Infrastructur=
e Debt Boom</h3>
            <p>Broadcom is negotiating a massive debt financing package of up=
 to $100 billion with private lenders, including Blackstone and Apollo. The f=
unds, raised through Special Purpose Vehicles (SPVs), will finance AI chips a=
nd infrastructure for Anthropic.</p>
            <p>This transaction reflects a broader trend where private credit=
 firms step in to fund the capital-intensive AI buildout. By utilizing SPVs, =
technology companies can secure essential hardware without directly impacting=
 their corporate balance sheets.</p>
            <p class=3D"trend-summary">So What? The rise of multi-billion dol=
lar private credit deals for AI infrastructure highlights a shift in technolo=
gy financing. Financial institutions must adapt to a landscape where private =
debt and structured finance play a dominant role in funding technological exp=
ansion.</p>
            <p><a href=3D"https://app.myzaker.com/news/article.php?pk=3D6a893=
b848e9f0921e71df54a">Read more</a></p>

            <h3>Trend 4: Off-Balance-Sheet Financing and the AI "Debt Bomb" D=
ebate</h3>
            <p>Financial analysts are raising concerns over a potential "debt=
 bomb" as major datacenter builders shift billions in liabilities off-balance=
-sheet. Tech giants like Meta, Oracle, and xAI are utilizing non-consolidated=
 special-purpose vehicles to fund construction.</p>
            <p>Reports indicate that tech companies shifted over $120 billion=
 of AI datacenter spending off their balance sheets by late 2025. While some =
skeptics draw comparisons to historical corporate failures like Enron, suppor=
ters argue the risks are broadly distributed.</p>
            <p class=3D"trend-summary">So What? The extensive use of off-bala=
nce-sheet financing masks the true capital intensity of the AI boom. Financia=
l analysts and auditors must carefully scrutinize corporate footnotes to accu=
rately assess the long-term risk exposure of major technology firms.</p>
